# Copy this file to .env, fill it in, and run `vp dev`. With none of the variables set the storefront
# runs against a stand-in for Kaiten. With the first three it runs against your Kaiten, and then:
#   - both slugs set: it signs in that one customer;
#   - neither slug set: visitors sign up (each makes a customer and an instance of their own, on your
#     catalogue's free plan: publish one, there is no sign-up without it), unless the publishable key
#     below opens the anonymous checkout, in which case a visitor buys with no account at all (see the
#     last section of this file);
#   - one slug alone: it refuses to start, and says which is missing.
# A variable of your shell wins over this file, even when it is empty. .env is git-ignored. Never prefix them
# with VITE_: these stay on the server. Vite expands $NAME in a value: write a literal $ as \$.

# Your Kaiten API's URL, /api included.
KAITEN_API_URL=

# A ksh_ service-account token. It never leaves the server. It holds write:customer_sessions and, to let
# visitors sign up (no slugs below), write:customers and write:instances too.
KAITEN_API_TOKEN=

# A publishable key (pk_) whose allowed origins include the storefront's, e.g. http://localhost:5173 (or the --port you use).
KAITEN_PUBLISHABLE_KEY=

# Optional, together with the next one: the slug of the customer the storefront signs in as.
# Leave both empty to let visitors sign up.
STOREFRONT_CUSTOMER_SLUG=

# Optional, together with the previous one: the slug of one of that customer's instances, the one a
# checkout subscribes.
STOREFRONT_INSTANCE_SLUG=

# Optional, sign-up mode: 32 characters or more, to sign the cookie that remembers a visitor's account.
# Without it a key is made at each start and the accounts are forgotten when the server restarts.
STOREFRONT_COOKIE_SECRET=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Visitors who buy with no account (nothing to set here: it is the publishable key's own setting)
#
# In your Kaiten, on the publishable key above:
#   - its allowed origins include this storefront's (http://localhost:5173, or the --port you use);
#   - the anonymous checkout is switched on, with a Cloudflare Turnstile site key and secret. The site key
#     is public and the catalogue hands it to the page; the secret is write-only and never leaves Kaiten.
#     Without a secret the key answers 422 TurnstileNotConfigured, with the switch off 403
#     AnonymousCheckoutDisabled.
#   - a plan that costs money needs a billing provider that captures payment methods, and its family a
#     fallback family with a free published version.
# The storefront reads the catalogue, and sells to a visitor with no account when it says the key may.
#
# For local use Cloudflare publishes test keys that need no account and no challenge:
#   site key  1x00000000000000000000AA              always passes
#   secret    1x0000000000000000000000000000000AA   accepts every token
# The others are for the failures. Site keys: 2x00000000000000000000AB always blocks, 3x00000000000000000000FF
# forces an interactive challenge. Secrets: 2x0000000000000000000000000000000AA always fails,
# 3x0000000000000000000000000000000AA answers that the token was already spent. Check them against
# Cloudflare's page on testing Turnstile before relying on them.
# Kaiten refuses with TurnstileFailed when the host Cloudflare reports for the widget is not the key's
# allowed origin. Which host the test secret reports is not confirmed: if it is a placeholder (example.com
# is the likely one) and not localhost, that host has to be an allowed origin of a key made for testing.
# Allow https://challenges.cloudflare.com in the page's Content-Security-Policy.
